    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0198",
      "sequence": 210,
      "meaning": "After reflection the guru gives the four names and declares that the king’s sons are the very essence of the Veda. The sage considers these children his wealth, people, entire possession and Śiva-like life. From infancy Lakṣmaṇa recognizes Rāma as his lord and gives his heart to Rāma’s feet, while Bharata and Śatrughna share the glorious love of lord and servant. The mothers gaze at the dark and fair pairs and ward away evil with blades of grass. All four are abodes of conduct, beauty and virtue, yet Rāma, ocean of joy, gives still greater delight. His enchanting smile shines like a moon revealing rays of inward grace. Sometimes Kauśalyā holds him at her breast, sometimes in a fine cradle, fondly calling him sweet child. The all-pervading, stainless, qualityless, birthless Brahman, beyond worldly play, rests in her lap, subject to love and devotion.",
